# ssh-copy-id — real-world examples

## Copy your default public key to a host

```sh
ssh-copy-id david@homelab
```

## Copy to a host on a non-standard SSH port

```sh
ssh-copy-id -p 2222 david@mediaserver
```

## Copy a specific (non-default) key

```sh
ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub david@homelab
```

## Specific key and non-standard port together

```sh
ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub -p 2222 david@mediaserver
```

## Flag reference

| Flag       | What it does                          |
| ---------- | ------------------------------------- |
| -i file    | public key to install                 |
| -p port    | connect on a non-standard SSH port    |
| -f         | force copy even if key seems present  |

> After copying, confirm with `ssh david@homelab` — it should not prompt for a
> password. On Tumbleweed `ssh-copy-id` ships with `openssh-clients`.
